The read-model doc drifted for as long as it existed, because nothing imports it
=================================================================================
Date : 2026-07-29
Mode : unit + tsc + next build (no runtime behaviour changed — this is a
       documentation/type file with zero importers)
Gate : make check 1528 -> 1533 (+5)

Found by : a forward sweep over the dashboard's own declared interfaces — the
           half of the codebase find_unconsumed_fields.py never looked at (it
           sweeps src/agents only).


What was wrong
----------------
`dashboard/src/lib/activity-model.ts` documents the DynamoDB single-table design
and exports record types plus four `make*Record` constructors.

Nothing imports it. Not one module. Rows are written by Python
(`ai/deploy_recorder.py`, `operations/activity_writer.py`) and read by
`activity-data.ts`, which maps untyped records defensively. So the file could
say anything and nothing would break — and it had drifted in both directions:

  AgentActivityRecord
    declared, produced by nobody : duration_ms, error_message
    produced, documented nowhere : trace, cost_metrics, deployment_id,
                                   instruction, model, summary, type
  DeploymentRecord
    produced, documented nowhere : cluster, type
  ProviderHealthRecord
    declared, produced by nobody : last_deployment_id, last_deployment_at

The three omissions on AgentActivityRecord are the three fields the deployment
detail page is built on, and `deployment_id` is the key that routes a row to
that page at all — the same field today's cost-panel fix turned on.

Two further claims in the file were false rather than merely stale:

  TTL. The file declared `ttl: number` (required) and stated "30 days for
  deployment/activity records". Only `activity_writer.py` writes a `ttl`.
  `deploy_recorder.py` — the agent path, which writes almost every row in
  practice — writes none, so those rows NEVER EXPIRE.

  GSI1. Declared required on both DEPLOY and ACTIVITY, with a documented access
  pattern "list deployments by provider". Again lambda-path only, and queried by
  nothing anywhere in the repo. A provider-scoped query written against this doc
  would have silently returned the lambda subset and missed every agent-written
  row — no error, just a short list.

There are TWO WRITER FAMILIES and neither is a superset of the other. The
documented model matched neither.


Why nothing caught it
-----------------------
`tests/test_dashboard_auth_and_activity.py::test_activity_model_schema` asserted
substring presence: `'GSI1PK:' in content`, `"TTL_30_DAYS" in content`,
`"makeDeploymentRecord" in content`. Keyword presence cannot see shape, so the
test passed for every one of the drifts above — it was green while the file
described a record that no writer had produced in a long time.

This is the anti-pattern this milestone already wrote down ("derive the guard,
do not enumerate it"), found in the test that was supposed to protect the file.


The fix
---------
1. `activity-model.ts` rewritten to describe what is actually written:
   * `*RecordCore` interfaces = the fields every writer of that PK emits;
     the full interfaces add per-writer-family optionals, each labelled with
     which family produces it.
   * The header now states plainly that the file is documentation with no
     importers, that GSI1 is half-populated and unqueried, and that agent-path
     rows never expire.
   * Access patterns are marked USED / NOT USED / NOT WRITTEN rather than
     listed as if all five existed.
   * The four `make*Record` constructors are removed. They were an unwired TS
     write path duplicating the Python writers — a second source of truth whose
     only possible future was to drift further apart. Nothing imported them.

2. `tests/test_activity_read_model_matches_writers.py` (5 tests) derives the
   real shapes from the writers' AST and asserts the two directions that matter:
   * no field is declared for a row type that no writer produces
     (catches `duration_ms`)
   * every field emitted by *all* writers of a row type appears in the
     documented core (catches the omissions)
   * `ttl` / `GSI1PK` / `GSI1SK` stay optional at EVERY declaration site while
     only one writer family emits them, and the doc keeps saying agent rows do
     not expire
   Plus a guard-the-guard: an AST walk that matched nothing would pass
   everything, so both writer families must be found.

   Deliberately NOT asserted: that the doc lists every optional field. Partial
   fields are real here, and demanding the union would make the doc restate the
   writers instead of explaining them.

3. The keyword test was replaced with the part of it that is genuinely a naming
   contract (the live PK values, and that HEALTH_HISTORY is labelled NOT
   WRITTEN), with the removed assertions and the reason recorded in its
   docstring.


Falsification (each applied alone; restored green after every one)
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  0. the original committed activity-model.ts ..... 3 failed / 2 passed
  1. reintroduce duration_ms ...................... 1 failed / 4 passed
  2. drop duration_sec from the documented core ... 1 failed / 4 passed
  3. make `ttl` required at one of its two sites .. 1 failed / 4 passed
  4. make `GSI1PK` required at one of two sites ... 1 failed / 4 passed
  restored ........................................ 5 passed

Revert 3 initially PASSED. The assertion used `re.search`, so one optional
declaration anywhere satisfied it while the other had been made required —
a guard that checked `any` where it meant `all`. Tightened to require every
declaration site, then both 3 and 4 fail as they should. Worth recording: the
first version of a guard written against a two-site field was itself the
half-covering kind of check this whole milestone is about.


Verification
--------------
  make check   1533 passed, 1 skipped   (1528 -> 1533, +5)
  npx tsc --noEmit                       clean
  npx next build                         success

No runtime behaviour changed — the module has no importers, which is the entire
point of the defect.
